@page "/blogs/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(ConfiguringAuthenticationInFunctionalTestingInASPNET)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
        <p>
            In this article, let's learn about how to setup <Highlight>Authentication</Highlight> in 
            <Highlight>Functional Test</Highlight> in <Highlight>WebAPI</Highlight> in ASP.NET Core on <Highlight>.NET 11</Highlight>.
        </p>

        <p>
            <strong>Note:</strong> If you have not done so already, I recommend you read the article on
            <NavLink href="blogs/using-wiremock-net-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi" Match="NavLinkMatch.All">Using WireMock.NET in Functional testing in ASP.NET WEB API</NavLink>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In our previous article we saw how to use <Highlight>WireMock.NET</Highlight> in functional test to validate HTTP calls. In this article,
            we will see how to setup <Highlight>Authentication</Highlight> and <Highlight>Authorization</Highlight> in functional test in 
            <Highlight>WebAPI</Highlight> in ASP.NET Core step by step. We will be using the same project that we used in our previous article.
        </p>

        </What>

        <Why>

        <p>
            The straight forward way to enable <Highlight>Authentication</Highlight> and <Highlight>Authorization</Highlight> in 
            Web API is to add <Highlight CssClasses="[ break-all ]">UseAuthentication()</Highlight> middleware and decorate necessary endpoints 
            with <Highlight>[Authorize]</Highlight> attribute.
        </p>

        <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer;

builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
        </CodeSnippet>

        <p>
            Use <Highlight>net11.0</Highlight> in both projects and <Highlight>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer 11.0.0</Highlight>
            in the API. Keep <Highlight>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing</Highlight> at <Highlight>11.0.0</Highlight> in the test project.
            Configure the real JWT bearer options for your issuer and audience in the application.
            The test-only handler below replaces authentication in the test host; it does not validate JWT signatures,
            issuers, audiences, or expiration. Keep real-token validation covered separately.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now we need to decorate the endpoints with <Highlight>[Authorize]</Highlight> attribute as shown below.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <caption>Decorating Endpoints with Authorize Attribute</caption>
            <img src="./image/blogs/webapi/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi/decorating-with-authorize.png" alt="decorating-with-authorize" class="[ w-full ]" />
        </figure>

        <p>
            Now if we run our tests, all our tests will fail with <Highlight>401 UnAuthorized</Highlight> as shown below.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <caption>Failing Tests after enabling Authentication</caption>
            <img src="./image/blogs/webapi/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi/failed-test.png" alt="failed-test" class="[ w-full ]" />
        </figure>

        <p>
            Lets quickly verify if we are getting <Highlight>401 UnAuthorized</Highlight> from swagger. Here is the swagger response from the 
            <Highlight>WeatherForecast</Highlight> endpoint.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <caption>Swagger output from Endpoint to return values from API</caption>
            <img src="./image/blogs/webapi/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi/swagger-response.png" alt="swagger-response" class="[ w-full ]" />
        </figure>

            <p>
                Adding <Highlight>[Authorize]</Highlight> to endpoints immediately breaks all functional tests because functional tests
                spin up the real ASP.NET Core pipeline — unlike unit tests, the authentication middleware is fully active. Without a proper
                fake authentication scheme, every protected endpoint returns <Highlight>401 UnAuthorized</Highlight>, making it impossible
                to test any secured functionality.
            </p>

            <p>
                To restore test coverage without compromising security, we need a structured fake authentication scheme that injects
                controllable claims and roles into the test pipeline, allowing each test to simulate different users and access levels.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4 id="setup-authentication">Setup Authentication</h4>

        <p>
            Setting up <Highlight>Authentication</Highlight> in <Highlight>Functional Test</Highlight> is a bit tricky as it cannot be 
            mocked like unit tests. We replace the authentication scheme in the test host as shown in the
            <NavLink href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/test/integration-tests?view=aspnetcore-11.0#mock-authentication" class="[ underline ]" target="_blank">official documentation</NavLink>.
            Keep the <Highlight>xUnit.net v3 AppFixture</Highlight> and per-test <Highlight>BaseTest</Highlight> from the previous article.
            Do not add a separate class or collection fixture.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Let's start with Empty <Highlight>AuthClaimsProvider</Highlight>.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Security.Claims;

namespace FunctionalTest;
public class AuthClaimsProvider
{
    public IList&lt;Claim&gt; Claims { get; }

    public AuthClaimsProvider(IList&lt;Claim&gt; claims)
    {
        Claims = claims;
    }

    public AuthClaimsProvider()
    {
        Claims = [];
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Next, add <Highlight>TestAuthHandler</Highlight> and the <Highlight>ClaimDto</Highlight> record shown below.
                    Claims from a request header override the default provider. Empty claims represent an anonymous request;
                    malformed headers fail authentication rather than falling back to an authenticated default user.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Options;
using System.Security.Claims;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Encodings.Web;
using System.Text.Json;

namespace FunctionalTest;
public class TestAuthHandler(
    IOptionsMonitor&lt;AuthenticationSchemeOptions&gt; options,
    ILoggerFactory logger,
    UrlEncoder encoder,
    AuthClaimsProvider claimsProvider) : AuthenticationHandler&lt;AuthenticationSchemeOptions&gt;(options, logger, encoder)
{
    public const string SchemeName = "Test";
    public const string ClaimsHeader = "X-Test-Claims";
    private readonly IList&lt;Claim&gt; _claims = claimsProvider.Claims;

    protected override Task&lt;AuthenticateResult&gt; HandleAuthenticateAsync()
    {
        IList&lt;Claim&gt; claims;

        if (Request.Headers.TryGetValue(ClaimsHeader, out var headerValue))
        {
            if (headerValue.Count != 1 || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(headerValue[0]))
            {
                return Task.FromResult(AuthenticateResult.Fail("Invalid test claims header."));
            }

            try
            {
                var json = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(Convert.FromBase64String(headerValue[0]!));
                var dtos = JsonSerializer.Deserialize&lt;ClaimDto[]&gt;(json);
                if (dtos is null || dtos.Any(d =&gt;
                    d is null || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(d.Type) || d.Value is null))
                {
                    return Task.FromResult(AuthenticateResult.Fail("Invalid test claims payload."));
                }

                claims = dtos.Select(d =&gt; new Claim(d.Type, d.Value)).ToList();
            }
            catch (FormatException)
            {
                return Task.FromResult(AuthenticateResult.Fail("Test claims must be Base64 encoded."));
            }
            catch (JsonException)
            {
                return Task.FromResult(AuthenticateResult.Fail("Test claims must contain valid JSON."));
            }
        }
        else
        {
            claims = _claims;
        }

        if (claims.Count == 0)
        {
            return Task.FromResult(AuthenticateResult.NoResult());
        }

        var identity = new ClaimsIdentity(claims, SchemeName);
        var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal(identity);
        var ticket = new AuthenticationTicket(principal, SchemeName);

        var result = AuthenticateResult.Success(ticket);

        return Task.FromResult(result);
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Add the following callback inside the existing <Highlight>AppFixture.ConfigureWebHost</Highlight>.
                    Keep its database registrations and WireMock configuration unchanged. Add the authentication, test-host,
                    dependency-injection, and claims namespaces shown here to <Highlight>AppFixture.cs</Highlight>.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using System.Security.Claims;

// Inside AppFixture.ConfigureWebHost, after the existing configuration:
builder.ConfigureTestServices(services =&gt;
{
    services.AddAuthentication(options =&gt;
        {
            options.DefaultScheme = TestAuthHandler.SchemeName;
            options.DefaultAuthenticateScheme = TestAuthHandler.SchemeName;
            options.DefaultChallengeScheme = TestAuthHandler.SchemeName;
            options.DefaultForbidScheme = TestAuthHandler.SchemeName;
        })
        .AddScheme&lt;AuthenticationSchemeOptions, TestAuthHandler&gt;(TestAuthHandler.SchemeName, _ =&gt; { });

    services.AddScoped(_ =&gt; new AuthClaimsProvider(
        [new Claim(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier, "default-test-user")]));
});
                </CodeSnippet>

            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Configure authentication scheme to use custom authentication handler (<Highlight>TestAuthHandler</Highlight>) that creates 
                    fake identity for integration tests. This handler creates fake user when <Highlight>HandleAuthenticateAsync()</Highlight> is 
                    called. We don't need any additional hacks to make ASP.NET Core application use this fake identity. Our integration test needs also some 
                    changes because of authentication handler.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Explicit defaults override applications that already set separate bearer authenticate or challenge schemes.
            They do not override an <Highlight>[Authorize(AuthenticationSchemes = "Bearer")]</Highlight> attribute or a policy
            that explicitly selects bearer authentication. Such endpoints need a matching test-host policy configuration
            or real test tokens. Register this handler only in the functional test project, never in the production host.
        </p>

        <p>
            That's it now if we run our tests, it should pass. Here is the output of the test after adding the above code.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <caption>Authorize Passing Test</caption>
            <img src="./image/blogs/webapi/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi/authorize-passing-test.png" alt="authorize-passing-test" class="[ w-full ]" />
        </figure>

        <h4 id="setup-authorization">Setup Authorization</h4>

        <p>
            The default provider supplies the same identity to clients without a claims header. The handler already supports
            per-request overrides; now we will create clients with different users and roles. This keeps claims local to each client
            without mutating the assembly fixture or starting another application host.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    To make test authentication handler support different user accounts I extended claims provider. This is the <Highlight>class</Highlight> 
                    that carries given set of <Highlight>claims</Highlight> to test authentication provider. I added also some static methods to 
                    return already initialized provider with set of claims specific for role like <Highlight>Guest</Highlight> and 
                    <Highlight>Admin</Highlight>.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Security.Claims;

namespace FunctionalTest;
public class AuthClaimsProvider
{
    public IList&lt;Claim&gt; Claims { get; }

    public AuthClaimsProvider(IList&lt;Claim&gt; claims)
    {
        Claims = claims;
    }

    public AuthClaimsProvider()
    {
        Claims = [];
    }

    public static AuthClaimsProvider WithGuestClaims()
    {
        var provider = new AuthClaimsProvider();
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier, Guid.NewGuid().ToString()));
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, "Guest User"));
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.Role, "Guest"));

        return provider;
    }

    public static AuthClaimsProvider WithAdminClaims()
    {
        var provider = new AuthClaimsProvider();
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier, Guid.Empty.ToString()));
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, "Admin User"));
        provider.Claims.Add(new Claim(ClaimTypes.Role, "Admin"));

        return provider;
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

                <p>
                    Replace the <Highlight>AuthClaimsProvider</Highlight> registration above with
                    <Highlight>services.AddScoped(_ =&gt; AuthClaimsProvider.WithGuestClaims());</Highlight>
                    to make ordinary clients authenticated guests. An empty provider passed to the client extension still sends
                    an empty claims array, which explicitly overrides the default and produces an anonymous request.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Now we need to add a <Highlight>ClaimDto</Highlight> record, a <Highlight>ClaimsInjectionHandler</Highlight> delegating 
                    handler, and a simplified extension method to create <Highlight>HttpClient</Highlight> with the required <Highlight>Claims</Highlight>.
                    Instead of spinning up a new <Highlight>TestServer</Highlight> via <Highlight CssClasses="[ break-all ]">WithWebHostBuilder</Highlight> 
                    for every test, we stamp an <Highlight>X-Test-Claims</Highlight> header on each request so that <Highlight>TestAuthHandler</Highlight> 
                    resolves per-request claims without any extra server initialization. The code for that is shown below.
                </p>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
namespace FunctionalTest;

internal sealed record ClaimDto(string Type, string Value);
                </CodeSnippet>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using System.Security.Claims;
using System.Text;
using System.Text.Json;

namespace FunctionalTest;

internal sealed class ClaimsInjectionHandler(IList&lt;Claim&gt; claims) : DelegatingHandler
{
    private readonly string _encoded = Convert.ToBase64String(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(
        JsonSerializer.Serialize(claims.Select(c =&gt; new ClaimDto(c.Type, c.Value)).ToArray())));

    protected override Task&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt; SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        request.Headers.Remove(TestAuthHandler.ClaimsHeader);
        request.Headers.Add(TestAuthHandler.ClaimsHeader, _encoded);
        return base.SendAsync(request, cancellationToken);
    }
}
                </CodeSnippet>

                <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;

namespace FunctionalTest;
public static class WebApplicationFactoryExtensions 
{
    public static HttpClient CreateClientWithClaim(this WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; factory, AuthClaimsProvider claims)
        =&gt; factory.CreateDefaultClient(new ClaimsInjectionHandler(claims.Claims));
}
                </CodeSnippet>

            </li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            That's it. Now we can use the above code to configure <Highlight>claims</Highlight> and <Highlight>roles</Highlight> for 
            each test. Let's try to test both <Highlight>Guest</Highlight> and <Highlight>Admin</Highlight> role.
        </p>

        <p>
            Now lets try to test the <Highlight>Admin</Highlight> role on <Highlight CssClasses="[ break-all ]">GetWeatherForecastFromDatabase</Highlight> 
            endpoint. Decorate that controller action with <Highlight>[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]</Highlight>;
            plain <Highlight>[Authorize]</Highlight> accepts any authenticated user and does not test role enforcement.
            Use the extension method to create <Highlight>HttpClient</Highlight> with <Highlight>Admin</Highlight> claims.
        </p>

        <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
using API;
using API.Services;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;
using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;
using System.Net;
using Xunit;

namespace FunctionalTest;
[ExcludeFromCodeCoverage]
public class WeatherForecastControllerShouldTests(
    AppFixture fixture,
    ITestOutputHelper outputHelper) : BaseTest(fixture)
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task ReturnExpectedResponse_From_Database()
    {
        var expectedWeatherForecast = await Database.WeatherForecasts.FirstAsync();

        using var client = Factory.CreateClientWithClaim(AuthClaimsProvider.WithAdminClaims());
        var result = await client.GetJsonResultAsync&lt;List&lt;WeatherForecast&gt;&gt;("/weatherforecast/fromdatabase", HttpStatusCode.OK, outputHelper);
        Assert.Equivalent(expectedWeatherForecast, Assert.Single(result));
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task RejectGuest_And_AnonymousRequests()
    {
        using var guest = Factory.CreateClientWithClaim(AuthClaimsProvider.WithGuestClaims());
        using var guestResponse = await guest.GetAsync("/weatherforecast/fromdatabase");
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.Forbidden, guestResponse.StatusCode);

        using var anonymous = Factory.CreateClientWithClaim(new AuthClaimsProvider());
        using var anonymousResponse = await anonymous.GetAsync("/weatherforecast/fromdatabase");
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.Unauthorized, anonymousResponse.StatusCode);
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task RejectMalformedClaims()
    {
        using var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, "/weatherforecast/fromdatabase");
        request.Headers.Add(TestAuthHandler.ClaimsHeader, "not-base64");
        using var response = await Client.SendAsync(request);
        Assert.Equal(HttpStatusCode.Unauthorized, response.StatusCode);
    }
}
        </CodeSnippet>

        <p>
            Thats it. We are done with setup. Now the tests will run in the following flow.
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Create the assembly's AppFixture and start its external services once.</li>
            <li>Run Program.cs.</li>
            <li>Add Default Authentication.</li>
            <li>Service Registration.</li>
            <li>Build Web Application.</li>
            <li>Apply migrations and seed data once.</li>
            <li>Create each test with the shared AppFixture and its own database scope.</li>
            <li>Create HTTP Client with required Claims.</li>
            <li>Test Code Execution.</li>
            <li>Dispose each test's clients and scope; dispose the fixture after all tests finish.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Now lets run the test and see the result.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <img src="./image/blogs/webapi/configuring-authentication-in-functional-testing-in-asp-net-webapi/all-passing-test.png" alt="all-passing-test" class="[ w-full ]" />
        </figure>

        <p>
            The expected results are <Highlight>200</Highlight> for an admin, <Highlight>403</Highlight> for an authenticated guest,
            and <Highlight>401</Highlight> for anonymous or malformed requests. These checks exercise the real authorization
            middleware with a test identity, not the production token-validation handler.
        </p>

        </How>

        <Summary>

        <p>
            In this article, we learnt about how to setup <Highlight>Authentication</Highlight> and <Highlight>Authorization</Highlight> 
            in <Highlight>Functional Test</Highlight> in <Highlight>WebAPI</Highlight> in ASP.NET Core. We learnt how to setup 
            <Highlight>Test Authentication Handler</Highlight> and <Highlight>Test Scheme</Highlight> and also learnt how to inject 
            different users and claims in runtime. The complete source code for this article can be found <NavLink href="https://github.com/fingers10/ILoveDotNetFunctionalTest" class="[ underline ]" target="_blank">here</NavLink>. 
            With this we are completing the series on <Highlight>Functional Testing in ASP.NET WebAPI</Highlight>. I'm happy to share my knowledge 
            on functional testing. Hope you enjoyed reading it.
        </p>
        </Summary>
